            "text": "{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"scope\",\n      \"location\": \"∀ n : ℕ, ∃ C : ℝ\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The proposition allows a different C for each n, while the informal problem requires one C independent of n.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"With the counting-function hypothesis dropped, some functions satisfy the pointwise bounds but admit no uniform C.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"separation\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": \"(f : ℕ → ℕ)\",\n      \"edit_from\": \"∀ n : ℕ, ∃ C : ℝ\",\n      \"edit_to\": \"∃ C : ℝ, ∀ n : ℕ\",\n      \"bridge\": \"intrinsic\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Separation drops hf and therefore shows only a logical-form difference; it does not establish that the counting function lacks a uniform bound.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"type\": \"domain\",\n      \"location\": \"n ≥ 2\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The informal claim covers positive integers, but the proposition excludes n = 1.\",\n      \"prediction\": \"Extending the stated bounds to n = 1 makes them impossible: the lower bound is positive and the upper bound is exp(-1/4) < 1, so neither possible natural-number value of f 1 can satisfy both.\",\n      \"probe_kind\": \"separation\",\n      \"comparison\": null,\n      \"witnesses\": \"(f : ℕ → ℕ)\",\n      \"edit_from\": \"n ≥ 2\",\n      \"edit_to\": \"n ≥ 1\",\n      \"bridge\": \"intrinsic\",\n      \"obligation\": \"Separation drops hf. The informal statement appears to need a restriction to n ≥ 2 or another adjustment at n = 1; this probe does not show the formal theorem is false.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The quantifier order loses the required uniform constant. The formal domain also omits n = 1, where the informal bounds as written cannot hold.\"\n}"
